      "content": "To help your students present a nuanced view, frame these counter-arguments by suggesting they focus on the \"market neutrality\" perspective. Proponents of this view often argue that in the absence of government intervention, the free market naturally corrects for inefficiencies and that individual choices, such as taking on high student debt or poor credit management, are the primary drivers of wealth accumulation.\n\nYou should guide your students to analyze the distinction between \"net neutral policies that have unintended consequences\" versus \"explicit policies designed to harm\" minority groups. This debate section should emphasize that the \"market forces\" argument often requires ignoring the historical context of how initial market conditions were skewed by institutional racism to be logically valid in these specific cases.",
